A||||22||KevinP||You won't believe this one........||||8||
Z||000000||KevinP||12-17-2000||07:53 PM||||OK, listen to my situation, I'm hoping someone can help me here.  Before I bought my '84, it was broken into and the lock on the driver side was punched, and so was the trunk.  So the only lock left on the car is the passenger side, which has periodically given me trouble.  So I go out to my car today, put the key in the lock, and turn.  Nothing happens.  I get no resistance when I turn the key.  After about a half hour of struggling and kicking, I call a campus saftey officer to try and slim-jim the lock open, but he has no success on either side.  So now I have to devise a method of getting into my car, hopefully without hurting my baby too badly.  Oh yeah, and I have to leave school for break on thursday morning, so I'm working against the clock too.  I will be forever grateful if the forum can pull through on this one!!  <P>Kevin<BR>locked out of an '84 SE||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||12-17-2000||07:57 PM||||Got a sunroof???||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||12-17-2000||08:07 PM||||lol, that was my thought too 56.<P>I had a similar problem with my car, when I bought it niether of the door locks matched the key I had.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||12-17-2000||08:14 PM||||The fiero has no window frame around the window so if you’re careful you can peel away the window from the gasket at the top and reach the lock or unlock button with a long stick, coat hanger, or something else. You may have to reach the opposite door to gain access or if you have power locks the unlock button.||||reg||1
Z||000004||KevinP||12-17-2000||08:30 PM||||I do have a sunroof, and I thought about that too, but the locking mechanism would never give.  I thought about making a hook out of a coat hangar and trying to slide the lock from the front.  I'll see how much give that window has tomorrow.  I appreciate the help you guys are giving, keep those ideas coming!<P>Kevin<BR>still locked out of an '84 SE||||reg||8
Z||000005||tgowens||12-17-2000||08:49 PM||||Kevin, it CAN be done with a coat hanger but you need to find something long and thin with a little stability -- if it is long enough to reach the other side of the car you can easily see what you are doing while sliding the locking mechanism open -- if not, try with a HEAVY wire coat hanger -- make a loop on the end for your hand (to turn and position the hanger once it is inside the car) and a small 90 degree bend on the end that goes inside the car -- it takes a lot of patience but it can be done - I have had to resort to that method a few times (either that or find one of the guys who hang out in the court house alley - I'll guarantee they will have it open in 12 seconds flat!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||12-17-2000||09:14 PM||||Sunroof takes 10 seconds to get in.<P>First make a fist with your hand. Then pinky side down(palm or whatever you call it, the soft side of your fist) slam your fist the glass just behind the two black caps for the latch. This will pop open the sunroof. Reach in, unlatch and lift out the glass. You're in, easy as can be.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||12-17-2000||09:41 PM||||I locked myself out once at a car show. The fella next to me had an "L" shaped rod-went in through the top of the window and had my car open faster than I could with a key.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Stoic||12-17-2000||09:51 PM||||I have locked my keys in my car many times.  I always take the same steps.  Press against the outside of the window (with palms).  try to gently slide it down a bit.  Even just a little will help.  Whether that helps or not, get a coat hanger and a piece of long thin wood. Stick the coat hanger in there and go for the window crank.  The window cranks down in a clockwise motion I believe.  You should be able to hook the crank and pull it to get you started.  Use the piece of wood for as the crank comes around and you have to push it down (hanger probably not strong enough).  That method has worked for me without fail and I've done it many times.  Now I have my keys hooked to my belt loop to prevent this.  It was becoming to much of a habit.  Good luck. <P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||6
Z||000009||KevinP||12-17-2000||10:03 PM||||wow, thanks for all the responses guys, i appreciate all your help on this.  I really like that window crank idea, I will give that a try tomorrow.  As for the sunroof, I'm still not sure how that works.  I just whack the glass and the latch underneath will pop?  I'll update everyone on any progress.  BTW, I'll obviously have to replace my locks now, anyone have any idea how hard it is to take the doors apart to get to the locks?<P>Kevin<BR>about to gain enterance to an '84 SE ||||reg||1
Z||000010||My7Fieros||12-17-2000||11:00 PM||||The coathanger thing over the window works..Ive done it before..just takes time and patience.<BR>Replacing the door locks is a serious PAIN in the A$$!! But, it obviously needs to be done..good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-17-2000||11:22 PM||||I know I'm going to make people groan with this one. I used to tow cars. I was cheap so I used to make my own lock out tools.<P>The most ridged wire I've ever found for this is the wire wreath tripods are made from. You can usually get leftover ones from a cemetary caretaker. You could get a new one at a florist.<P>That or buy some 1/2" strap iron.<P>Bend one end 90 and reach in over the window to get at the lock button. It's easier if someone can help guide you from the oposite side of the car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||12-17-2000||11:52 PM||||The sunroof thing won't work if the latch is really stiff. <P>Make a fist. Then turn your fist so that you can see your fingers curling into your palm. Your pinky finger should be on the bottom. Now, slam you hand onto the sunroof glass fairly hard just behind the bolts for the latch(towards the rear of the car). Since you're not hitting an unsupported area of the glass, chances of it breaking are slim.<P>I locked myself out of my Formula once, and the sunroof thing was just as fast, if not faster, than using my keys to get in.||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||12-18-2000||01:33 AM||||Being on many Fiero caravans, the easiest and most effective lock out tool (if you have a Fiero buddy with you) is the bar holding the spare tire down! Carefully pull the window back and hold the loop end, the curve at the other end is just perfect! <P>Unfortunately it only works if you have someone with their hood up...||||reg||12
Z||000014||Standard||12-18-2000||02:04 AM||||The one time I locked my keys in my car before I had a spare made, I was able to pull my window glass away from the car far enough to get my arm down and unlock the door.  Of course, my door wasn't shut all the way so that made it a bit easier.  Be very careful if you have to do this, as you can shatter the window pretty easily..  I've heard about the sunroof thing, too.  <P>I've never tried this on a Fiero, but you might be able to use the antenna, if it's not on that tight.  Unscrew it, then stick it in at the backside of the window so you can push the lock forward.  Had to do it on a Ford van that we locked the keys in once.. that was fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroSTETZ||12-18-2000||03:22 AM||||stick a ruler (wooden) in at the top, then twist it, should give you about 2 inches of room to work with (on side windows, so its not squishing against whatever you are trying to do) then point your fingers out straight, and weasel your arm down to the lock. i have done this 2 times, took about 5 minuts.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460757&p=25516673&Sequence=0&res=high"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Archie||12-18-2000||07:33 AM||||Get someone who actually knows how to use a SlimJim.<P>Everyone has tools that work.<P>Not everyone knows how to work his tools.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||overlord||12-18-2000||08:51 AM||||do you have ppower windows? I locked myslef out once, and couldnt get locks open, so i used the wire and rolled the window down enough so i could get my  hand in.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Tigger||12-18-2000||09:36 AM||||Don't think the power windows In my Fiero work without the ignition in the on position.<P>Still is a good idea if you happen to leave your keys in your Fiero with it running.||||reg||1
Z||000019||BOILERMAKER||12-18-2000||12:01 PM||||For someone who is under such constraints, if you just want to break the sunroof, I could get you one for a HECK of a deal.  <P>LMK||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||12-18-2000||04:00 PM||||I locked myself out about a year and a half ago and asked for ideas after I found that the coat hanger method didn't work (too flimsy). Ogre said about the same thing to me  then as he posted here. Instead of raiding the local graveyard, I found a 3 foot long piece of metal (steel) dowel rod (1/8" diameter I think) at the hardware store and bent a slight curve in it. I carefully pryed the top corner of the window back and wedged a small piece of wood in, then guided the rod into position and pushed the lock slide button. It was easy.||||reg||1
Z||000021||KevinP||12-18-2000||04:40 PM||||SUCCESS!! I searched through my dorm, and managed to scare up the strongest coat hangar I could find, and stole a broom from the janitor's closet.  I poked and prodded the slider for a bit, but the hangar would just flex and couldn't budge it.  As plan B I went for the crank and managed to open the window a crack, the shoved the broomstick into the space for more room.  I just cranked the window a little at a time until I could squeeze my arm in and slide the lock.  Thanks guys, this forum is the best.  So now, does anyone have any advice for replacing my locks?  <P>Kevin<BR>once again driving an '84 SE||||reg||10
Z||000022||BOILERMAKER||12-18-2000||05:31 PM||||Small hands and a long pair of needle nose is how I did mine.<P>You know what they say, small hands ... small gloves ... isn't that what they say?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
